    <!-- Body -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          A whole house UPS is a home battery system that switches over in about 20 milliseconds or less, fast enough that the house never goes dark. It is the best answer for short outages and the wrong one for a week without power.
        </p>

        <TopPick />

        <div id="what-it-means" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. What People Mean by a Whole House UPS
          </h2>

          <p>
            Nobody sells a product called a whole house UPS, but the phrase describes something real. A UPS, an uninterruptible power supply, is the battery box under a desk that keeps a computer running when the power blinks. A whole house UPS is the same idea scaled up: a battery system wired into the home's electrical panel that takes over the instant the grid drops, fast enough that the lights do not go out and the clocks do not reset. In practice that means a home battery system with an automatic transfer device, not an oversized computer UPS.
          </p>

          <p>
            The products that fit the description fall into two groups. The first is permanently installed home batteries: the Tesla Powerwall 3, the Enphase IQ Battery 5P and the FranklinWH aPower 2, each mounted on a wall or pad and wired into the panel by an electrician, often alongside solar. The second is large portable power stations that connect to a dedicated panel: the EcoFlow DELTA Pro Ultra with the Smart Home Panel 2, and the Anker SOLIX F3800 Plus with Anker's Home Power Panel. Both groups switch over automatically; they differ in capacity, output, cost and how permanent the installation is.
          </p>

          <p>
            What none of them are is a true online UPS in the data-center sense. An online, or double-conversion, UPS runs its load from the inverter all the time, so there is no transfer at all. Home batteries instead watch the grid and isolate the house when it fails, which takes a few milliseconds. For almost everything in a home that distinction does not matter, and the next section explains where it does.
          </p>

          <p>
            It is also worth separating this question from the product rankings. If you already know you want a home battery and are choosing between models, <a href="/best-home-battery-backup-system/">our home battery backup roundup</a> compares them in detail. This page is about the decision before that: whether a whole-house battery is the right tool for your outages at all, how big it needs to be, and when a standby generator or a small plug-in UPS is the better answer.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="switchover" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Switchover Time: Seamless, 20 Milliseconds, or 20 Seconds
          </h2>

          <p>
            The switchover is the feature that makes a battery system feel like a UPS, and it is the biggest practical difference from a generator. When the grid fails, the system has to detect it, open a relay that disconnects the house from the utility, and start supplying power from its own inverter. Manufacturers describe that delay in different ways. FranklinWH lists a switchover time of under 16 milliseconds from grid to backup. EcoFlow lists 20 milliseconds for the Smart Home Panel 2, and retailer listings give 20 milliseconds for Anker's F3800 with its Home Power Panel. Tesla describes the Powerwall 3 with Backup Gateway 2 as a seamless transition without publishing a millisecond figure.
          </p>

          <p>
            Twenty milliseconds is about one cycle of 60 Hz power. Lights may flicker or not visibly change. Refrigerators, furnaces, pumps and most electronics carry on. Desktop computer power supplies are generally designed to ride through a gap of around that length, though an older or heavily loaded one may not, and some devices with sensitive clocks, such as certain microwaves or older digital displays, may still reset. If a specific device must never see a gap, a small plug-in UPS on that device is the real fix.
          </p>

          <p>
            A standby generator works on a completely different timescale. Its automatic transfer switch waits a few seconds to confirm the outage is real, starts the engine, lets it stabilize, then transfers the house. Most residential standby systems restore power somewhere around 10 to 30 seconds after the grid drops, and the delay is adjustable; Champion, for example, lists a response time of 2.5 to 240 seconds on its 26kW fleX transfer switch. During that gap the house is dark, the Wi-Fi drops and every clock resets.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Backup type</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Typical gap</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">What you notice</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Online (double-conversion) UPS</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">None</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Nothing; used for servers and lab equipment</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Home battery with automatic transfer</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Under 16 to about 20 ms, by maker</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">A flicker at most; most devices keep running</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Line-interactive plug-in UPS</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">A few milliseconds</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Nothing on the protected device</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Standby generator with ATS</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Roughly 10 to 30 seconds</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">A short blackout, then power returns</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Portable generator with transfer switch</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Minutes</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Someone has to go outside and start it</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            For most households the gap is a convenience question, not a safety one. A 20-second blackout does not hurt a refrigerator, a furnace or a sump pump. It does interrupt home offices, medical equipment and anything with an unsaved state. That is where a battery's switchover earns its keep, and where pairing a generator with a small UPS on the critical device is often the cheaper solution.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="specs" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. The Systems, by Capacity and Continuous Output
          </h2>

          <p>
            Two numbers define what a whole-house battery can do. Capacity, in kilowatt-hours, decides how long it lasts. Continuous output, in kilowatts, decides how much it can run at once, including whether it can start a central air conditioner or a well pump. Buyers fixate on the first and get caught by the second: a battery with plenty of stored energy and too little output trips the moment two big motors start together.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">System</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Capacity per unit</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Continuous output</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Switchover (as listed)</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Tesla Powerwall 3</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">13.5 kWh</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">11.5 kW (North America)</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Described as seamless</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Enphase IQ Battery 5P</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">5.0 kWh</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">3.84 kVA (7.68 kVA peak, 3 s)</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Requires IQ System Controller 3/3G</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">FranklinWH aPower 2 + aGate X</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">15 kWh</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">10 kW (15 kW peak, 10 s)</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Under 16 ms</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">EcoFlow DELTA Pro Ultra + Smart Home Panel 2</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">6 kWh per battery, to 90 kWh</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">7.2 kW per inverter</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">20 ms</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Anker SOLIX F3800 Plus + Home Power Panel</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">3.84 kWh</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">6 kW, 120/240V</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">20 ms (retailer listing)</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            The Tesla Powerwall 3 has the highest single-unit output on this list at 11.5 kW continuous on its North American datasheet, because its solar inverter is built in. That headroom is why one unit can often start a central air conditioner without a soft starter. The FranklinWH aPower 2 holds the most energy per cabinet at 15 kWh with 10 kW continuous, and its aGate X controller can also start and manage a standby generator, which matters later in this guide. The Enphase IQ Battery 5P is smaller, 5 kWh and 3.84 kVA per unit, and is designed to be stacked; three of them give 15 kWh and about 11.5 kVA.
          </p>

          <p>
            The portable-based systems trade permanence for flexibility. The EcoFlow DELTA Pro Ultra starts at one 7.2 kW inverter with one 6 kWh battery, and EcoFlow says it scales to 90 kWh with three inverters and fifteen batteries, connected to the house through the Smart Home Panel 2. The Anker SOLIX F3800 Plus is a 3.84 kWh, 6,000-watt unit with native 120/240-volt output that can roll to wherever it is needed and connects through Anker's Home Power Panel. Both sit closer to <a href="/best-portable-power-station-for-home-backup/">portable power stations for home backup</a> than to a Powerwall, and both can be added to over time.
          </p>

          <p>
            All of these figures are manufacturer specifications. Continuous output often depends on configuration, temperature and, for some systems, whether solar is producing at the time. Enphase's UK variant of the 5P, for example, is rated lower than the US unit. Read the datasheet for the exact model and region on your quote, not a summary.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="sizing" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Sizing kWh Against a Day of Essentials
          </h2>

          <p>
            Size a whole-house battery the way you would plan a trip: by how much you use per day, then how many days. The mistake is to size against the house's total consumption. A typical American home uses something like 25 to 35 kWh a day, and a large share of that is air conditioning, electric heat, water heating and cooking. Backing up all of that for a day takes two or three Powerwall-sized batteries. Backing up the essentials takes far less.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">A day of essentials, with gas heat</h3>

          <p>
            Here is a realistic essentials budget for a gas-heated house, using typical appliance figures. A modern refrigerator uses about 1 to 2 kWh a day and a chest freezer about 1. A gas furnace's blower and controls draw roughly 400 to 800 watts while running; at eight to twelve hours of run time on a cold day, that is 3 to 8 kWh, and our <a href="/can-a-portable-power-station-run-a-gas-furnace/">guide to running a gas furnace on a power station</a> covers it in detail. Lighting, the internet router, phones and a television add 1 to 2 kWh. A well pump adds 1 to 2 kWh, and a sump pump in a storm can add several more.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-4">Estimated Daily Use, Essentials Only</p>
            <div class="grid grid-cols-2 md:grid-cols-4 gap-4 text-center">
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">2-3 kWh</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Refrigerator and freezer</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">3-8 kWh</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Gas furnace blower, winter day</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">1-2 kWh</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Lights, router, TV, phones</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">8-15 kWh</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Typical essentials total</p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            That puts a day of essentials somewhere around 8 to 15 kWh. One 13.5 kWh Powerwall 3 or one 15 kWh aPower 2 covers that for roughly a day, and two cover two days. A single 3.84 kWh F3800 Plus covers a refrigerator, freezer, lights and router for most of a day, but not a furnace through a cold night as well. These are planning estimates, not measurements, so check your own appliance labels or a utility usage report.
          </p>

          <p>
            Central air conditioning changes the math completely. A 3-ton air conditioner draws around 3 kW while running, so ten hours of cooling on a hot afternoon and evening adds about 30 kWh, more than two Powerwalls hold. That is why whole-house battery quotes in hot climates almost always include either solar to recharge during the day, load management to keep the AC off backup, or both. If you need full air conditioning through a multi-day outage, keep reading, because that is where batteries stop being the sensible answer.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="cost" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. What a Whole House Battery Costs
          </h2>

          <p>
            Installed home batteries are expensive, and published prices vary widely by installer and region. For the Tesla Powerwall 3, the figure most often cited, attributed to EnergySage marketplace data, is about $13,500 for one installed unit before incentives, with many installer estimates falling between roughly $11,500 and $16,500. A panel upgrade, if needed, can add more. Get several quotes; installer pricing in the same market can differ substantially.
          </p>

          <p>
            The portable-based systems are cheaper to enter and easier to grow. At launch, EcoFlow priced the DELTA Pro Ultra with one inverter and one 6 kWh battery at $5,799 and the Smart Home Panel 2 at $1,899, before installation of the panel by an electrician. These systems cost less per installation but usually more per kWh of storage than a large installed battery once you add enough units to match one.
          </p>

          <p>
            Incentives have shifted. The 30% federal residential clean energy credit that subsidized many home battery purchases ended for homeowner-owned systems installed after 2025, so check what your state, utility or a third-party ownership arrangement offers before assuming a discount. Some utilities pay battery owners to share stored energy during peak demand, which changes the payback but also means the battery may not be full when an outage starts unless you reserve capacity for backup.
          </p>

          <p>
            For comparison, a whole-house standby generator, which runs a whole house for as long as it has fuel, typically lands in a similar total budget once installed. Our <a href="/generac-generator-cost/">Generac generator cost guide</a> and the <a href="/best-26kw-generator/">26kW generator guide</a> break down standby pricing. The choice between the two is less about the purchase price than about what kind of outages you get.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="vs-generator" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Whole House Battery Backup vs a Generator for Multi-Day Outages
          </h2>

          <p>
            Here is the honest comparison. A whole-house battery is better for short outages, and a standby generator is better for long ones. For an outage of a few hours, which is what most households see most often, a battery is silent, needs no fuel, produces no exhaust, switches over without a blackout and costs almost nothing to run. A generator does the same job with noise, a 10 to 30-second gap, fuel use and an engine that needs annual maintenance.
          </p>

          <p>
            The balance flips after roughly a day. A battery holds a fixed amount of energy, and once it is empty the house goes dark unless solar or a generator refills it. Solar recharging helps a great deal in summer and in sunny climates, but in a winter storm with snow on the panels, or under days of heavy cloud after a hurricane, solar production can fall to a small fraction of normal. A natural gas standby generator, by contrast, runs until the gas utility stops, which in most outages it does not; a propane unit on a 500-gallon tank runs for days.
          </p>

          <p>
            Air conditioning and electric heat are the other dividing line. A battery can run central air for a few hours. A 26kW generator can run two central air conditioners for a week. If your outages come with heat waves or deep cold and you rely on electric heat pumps, a battery-only system needs to be very large, very expensive, or paired with something else. Our <a href="/portable-power-station-vs-generator/">power station vs generator comparison</a> covers the same trade-off at the portable scale.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE RULE</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Short, frequent outages favor a battery. Long, rare ones favor a generator.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">If most of your outages last under a day, a battery covers them silently. If you plan for multi-day storms with air conditioning or electric heat, a standby generator is the cheaper way to keep the whole house running.</p>
          </div>

          <p>
            For households that get both, a hybrid is often the best answer. The FranklinWH aGate X is designed to manage a battery and a standby generator together: the battery carries short outages silently, and the generator starts automatically only when the battery drops to a set level. That keeps the generator's run hours and fuel use low and still covers a week-long outage. Our <a href="/best-whole-house-generator/">whole house generator guide</a> covers the generator side.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="small-ups" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. When a Small Plug-In UPS Is All You Need
          </h2>

          <p>
            Many people searching for a whole house UPS actually want something much smaller: the router, modem and a desktop computer to keep working through a brief flicker, or a few minutes to save work and shut down. That is the job of an ordinary plug-in UPS, and it costs a tiny fraction of a home battery. A 1,500VA line-interactive unit keeps a desktop and monitor running for several minutes and a router and modem for considerably longer. <a href="/best-ups-battery-backup/">Our UPS battery backup guide</a> compares the models.
          </p>

          <p>
            A small UPS also solves the one problem a standby generator cannot: the 10 to 30-second gap. Put a UPS on the home office, the network gear and any medical device that cannot lose power, and pair it with a generator for everything else. The UPS bridges the gap, the generator takes over, and the house never notices. That combination costs far less than a whole-house battery and covers multi-day outages that a battery cannot.
          </p>

          <p>
            Some needs sit in between. A sump pump is a common one: a dedicated battery backup sump pump covers the most damaging failure in a flooded basement for a modest cost, and <a href="/sump-pump-battery-backup-vs-generator/">our sump pump backup comparison</a> explains when that is enough. A refrigerator and a few lights can run from a portable power station plugged in by hand. Neither is automatic for the whole house, but both are far cheaper than wiring a battery into the panel.
          </p>

          <p>
            Buy a true whole-house battery when the automatic, silent switchover matters for the entire house, when outages are mostly short, and especially when you have or plan solar to refill it. Buy a standby generator when outages run for days and you need air conditioning or heat. Buy a small UPS when the real worry is a handful of devices. Most people who search the phrase need the third, and a few need the first two together.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            8. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Sizing by kWh and Ignoring Continuous Output</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A battery with plenty of stored energy still trips if two motors start at once and its inverter cannot supply them. Check continuous and peak output against your largest loads, especially a central air conditioner or a well pump, before comparing capacity. A 3.84 kVA unit and an 11.5 kW unit behave very differently.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Sizing Against the Whole House Instead of Essentials</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Backing up 30 kWh a day of total use costs two or three large batteries. Backing up 8 to 15 kWh of essentials takes one. Decide which circuits really need to stay on during an outage, then size the battery for those, with a backed-up subpanel or load control if needed.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Expecting Solar to Refill It in a Winter Storm</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Solar recharge makes a battery last for days in sunny weather. Under snow or a week of heavy cloud, production can fall to a fraction of normal. If your worst outages come in winter storms, plan capacity or a generator for days with little or no solar input.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Letting the Utility Program Drain Your Backup</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Utility programs that pay you to discharge a battery at peak times are worth having, but the battery may be half empty when the storm hits. Set a backup reserve in the app, and raise it before a forecast storm, so the energy you paid for is there when the grid fails.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying a Home Battery When a Plug-In UPS Would Do</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                If the real need is keeping a router, a desktop computer or a medical device alive through a flicker, a plug-in UPS does it for a tiny fraction of the cost. Paired with a standby generator, it covers both the brief gap and the multi-day outage.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            9. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is there such a thing as a whole house UPS?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Not as a single product name, but home battery systems with automatic transfer do the job. The Tesla Powerwall 3, Enphase IQ Battery 5P, FranklinWH aPower 2 and power-station systems like the EcoFlow DELTA Pro Ultra with Smart Home Panel 2 all take over automatically when the grid fails, quickly enough that most devices keep running.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How fast does a whole home battery backup switch over?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Manufacturer figures run from under 16 milliseconds for FranklinWH to 20 milliseconds for EcoFlow's Smart Home Panel 2; Tesla describes the Powerwall 3 transition as seamless. That is about one cycle of AC power, so lights may flicker but most appliances and electronics continue without interruption.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How long will a whole house battery last in an outage?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It depends on what runs. A day of essentials in a gas-heated home, meaning refrigerator, freezer, furnace blower, lights and internet, is roughly 8 to 15 kWh, so one 13.5 to 15 kWh battery covers about a day. Running central air conditioning can use 30 kWh or more in a day.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is a whole house battery better than a generator?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For short outages, yes: it is silent, needs no fuel and switches over without a blackout. For multi-day outages, especially with air conditioning or electric heat, a standby generator is usually better because it runs as long as it has fuel. Many homes pair a battery with a generator for both.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How much does a whole house battery backup cost?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                The most-cited figure for one installed Tesla Powerwall 3, attributed to EnergySage data, is about $13,500 before incentives, with many quotes between roughly $11,500 and $16,500. Power-station systems start lower; EcoFlow launched the DELTA Pro Ultra at $5,799 and the Smart Home Panel 2 at $1,899.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can a whole house battery run central air conditioning?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Some can. The Powerwall 3's 11.5 kW continuous output can typically start and run a central air conditioner, and the FranklinWH aPower 2 is rated 10 kW continuous. Running it is the bigger issue: a 3-ton system uses about 3 kW, so a single battery runs it for only a few hours alongside other loads.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do I need solar for a whole house battery?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                No. Every system here can charge from the grid and hold that energy for an outage. Solar is what lets a battery recharge during a long outage, so without it the battery's capacity is the limit. For multi-day outages without solar, a generator or a generator-battery hybrid is the more reliable plan.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

