        <!-- Closing -->
        <div class="mt-16 bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-8">
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            A whole house UPS is a home battery with an automatic transfer switch, and the good ones switch over in 20 milliseconds or less. Size it against a day of essentials, about 8 to 15 kWh for a gas-heated home, check its continuous output against your largest motor, and it will carry most short outages without the house noticing.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            For multi-day outages with air conditioning or electric heat, a standby generator remains the cheaper way to keep the whole house running, and a battery-generator hybrid gets you both. If all you need is a router and a computer to survive a flicker, skip both and buy a plug-in UPS.
          </p>
        </div>

